1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,237
AI agents are starting to
earn and spend real money.

2
00:00:04,237 --> 00:00:07,627
But an agent's money has nowhere to live.

3
00:00:07,627 --> 00:00:12,712
It sits in a raw wallet,
earning nothing, governed by nothing —

4
00:00:12,712 --> 00:00:19,492
because a private key is all-or-nothing. Ovolyn is
a bank account built for an autonomous depositor.

5
00:00:19,492 --> 00:00:25,000
I've just started a live run on
Arc; let's talk while it settles.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,652
One account on Arc, four flows.
Deposit USDC from any chain over CCTP.

7
00:00:30,652 --> 00:00:33,696
Idle balance sweeps into
tokenized treasury yield.

8
00:00:33,696 --> 00:00:40,652
A human sets the policy. And the agent
spends inside it, on its own, over x402.

9
00:00:40,652 --> 00:00:44,130
It is built on Circle's own rails —

10
00:00:44,130 --> 00:00:55,000
Agent Stack for the wallet, Gateway for settlement, CCTP for the deposit,
App Kit for currency, and USYC for the yield. Nothing here is simulated.

11
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,439
Deposit landed — three USDC
burned on Sepolia, minted on Arc.

12
00:00:59,439 --> 00:01:08,719
Thirty-four seconds, and I want to be precise about where that goes: Circle's
fast attestation waits two Ethereum confirmations — Sepolia's twelve-second slots.

13
00:01:08,719 --> 00:01:12,351
The standard path would be
fifteen to nineteen minutes.

14
00:01:12,351 --> 00:01:15,175
Arc's own blocks are half a second.

15
00:01:15,175 --> 00:01:18,000
Every hash here is live on Arcscan.

16
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:27,273
The balance crossed the idle threshold, so the
account swept the excess into USYC — tokenized treasury.

17
00:01:27,273 --> 00:01:31,636
You can see the exact
amount minted, on-chain.

18
00:01:31,636 --> 00:01:36,000
The war chest earns
while the agent sleeps.

19
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:45,804
Then it spent. A gas oracle priced at a tenth of a cent, bought
from an independent merchant, settled through Gateway — 402 to 200.

20
00:01:45,804 --> 00:01:54,039
These listings are open: anyone lists by signing with the
address they want paid at, and an automated probe admits them.

21
00:01:54,039 --> 00:01:56,000
There is no approval queue.

22
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,500
And then it tried to
spend five cents. Refused.

23
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:08,000
The policy caps any single transaction at
one cent, so the payment never happened —

24
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:17,000
and the refusal is written into the ledger with its
reason, right next to the payments that succeeded.

25
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:22,000
A bank that only records
what succeeded isn't a bank.

26
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:24,847
That boundary is what makes autonomy safe.

27
00:02:24,847 --> 00:02:30,136
Given an open goal, the agent bought
the three services it could afford.

28
00:02:30,136 --> 00:02:37,864
When the deepest analysis came in over the limit,
it didn't overreach and it didn't quietly drop it —

29
00:02:37,864 --> 00:02:43,966
it stopped and asked me to raise the
limit, naming the service and the number.

30
00:02:43,966 --> 00:02:46,000
It asked, rather than took.

31
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,375
Authority is created by humans;
permission is derived to machines.

32
00:02:50,375 --> 00:02:54,750
Built on Arc, with Circle's
stack. Agentic Economy and DeFi.

33
00:02:54,750 --> 00:03:00,000
We monetize the balance sheet —
payments are why the balance exists.
